          <p>
            Spend on a good point, a length suited to your height and setup, and a handle material that fits how you store tools. Those three choices matter far more than brand. Pair a hookaroon with a good splitter and saw, and the whole firewood job becomes easier on your back.
          </p>

          <p>
            One last point on value: a hookaroon is not a substitute for good body mechanics or a sensible pace. Take breaks, rotate tasks between splitting, hauling and stacking, and stop when you are tired. Most strains at the woodpile happen late in a long session, when form slips and people start lifting with their backs.
          </p>
