        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying Too Short a Tool</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Short tools are quick at the splitter but require bending to reach rounds on the ground, which defeats much of the purpose. Unless you work exclusively at waist height, choose a hookaroon in the upper 20-inch range, or own both a short and a long tool for different jobs.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Swinging Too Hard</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A sharp point needs only a light swing to set. Big overhead strikes waste energy, bury the point too deeply, and increase the chance of glancing off and hitting your leg or foot. Use a short, controlled motion and keep the point sharp so it sets easily.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using It While the Splitter Ram Moves</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Reaching in with a hookaroon while the ram is extending is as dangerous as reaching in with your hand, since the tool can be caught and wrenched toward you. Wait until the ram stops or fully retracts before setting the point to clear or position wood.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Prying With the Point</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Using the hookaroon as a pry bar bends points and breaks handles, especially wood ones. To free a stuck point, rock it gently in line with the curve. Use a cant hook or peavey for rolling and prying logs, which is what those tools are designed for.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving It Lying in the Grass</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A hookaroon left point-up in grass or leaves is easy to step on or trip over, and wood handles left in wet grass swell and loosen. Hang it point-to-the-wall or cover the point, and store it under cover and out of reach of children. A wall hook costs a few dollars.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

