        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What do people actually do with a hookaroon?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is used to grab, drag, lift, flip and toss rounds and split firewood without bending over or putting hands near rough bark and splinters. Most owners use it to feed a log splitter, pull split halves off the beam, move rounds from a pile and stack splits. It is a firewood handling tool, not a tool for rolling large logs.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a pickaroon different from a hookaroon?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The names are often used interchangeably. Where people distinguish them, a pickaroon usually has a shorter, more axe-like head with a straighter spike, while a hookaroon has a more pronounced curved point for pulling. In practice, manufacturers choose one name or the other, and length and point shape matter more than the label.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What length hookaroon is best?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most homeowners, a tool in the upper 20-inch range is the best single choice, since it reaches the ground from a nearly upright stance and still works at the splitter. Shorter tools around 15 to 18 inches are quick at the splitter but need more bending. Taller users benefit most from longer handles.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I still need a cant hook if I own a hookaroon?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, if you buck your own logs. A cant hook has a long handle and hinged hook for rolling logs on the ground while bucking them into rounds. A hookaroon is for moving rounds and splits. They do different jobs, and people who cut their own trees usually own both, or choose a multi-tool such as the LogOX that combines functions.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I sharpen a hookaroon point?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Use a mill file, following the existing profile and filing toward the tip, keeping the point slim and symmetrical. Avoid overheating the steel on a bench grinder, which can ruin its temper. Clean and lightly oil the head afterward to prevent rust. Touch up the point whenever it starts glancing off wood instead of setting easily.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I choose wood or composite handles?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Hickory is strong, absorbs shock and is replaceable, but it needs oiling and storage out of the weather. Composite and fiberglass handles shrug off rain, need no maintenance and resist overstrike damage. Aluminum is lightest. Choose based on how you store tools; if they live near the woodpile, composite or aluminum is the easier choice.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a hookaroon work on frozen or green wood?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, though it behaves differently. Green wood is softer and the point sets easily, but it can grip deeply and need a twist to free. Frozen wood is harder, so the point may glance off; a sharp tip and a deliberate, controlled swing help. Be careful on icy ground, where pulling on a stuck tool can lead to a fall.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

